welcome to the quick start for cloud  storage google cloud's object store for  unstructured data such as images videos  and documents  in this video we're going to walk  through how to perform basic tasks in  cloud storage using the gsutil command  line tool  to start visit console.cloud.google.com  to open the google cloud console  this web portal lets you manage and  explore your cloud resources directly  from a browser  to get started with cloud storage you  need a google cloud project you can use  an existing project or set up a new one  next activate cloud shell by clicking  its icon to the right of the search bar  cloud shell is an online development  environment that comes pre-loaded with  helpful tools for interacting with your  cloud resources  once it launches click the maximize  button to increase the size of the cloud  shell terminal  now you'll run some commands using the  gsutil command line tool which allows  you to interact with your cloud storage  resources from command line  gsutil stands for google storage utility  now create a cloud storage bucket  buckets are the basic containers that  hold your data in cloud storage  to create a bucket run the gsutil mb  command and provide a global unique name  for your bucket  this demo uses a bucket named storage  quick start dash 777 but you must choose  your own bucket name  next upload an object into your bucket  first run the wget command to download a  kitten.png image to the cloud shell  environment then run the gsutil cb  command to copy the image from your  local cloud shell environment to your  cloud storage bucket  congratulations you've successfully  uploaded an object to your bucket  now download the object from cloud  storage back to your local cloud shell  environment using the gsutil cp command  again  you'll download the object as a file  called kitten2.png  if you run ls you can see that it was  downloaded successfully  within cloud storage buckets you can  create folders to organize your objects  with gsutil cp you can upload an object  to a specific folder in a cloud storage  bucket  this command creates a folder called  folder 1 within your bucket and uploads  the kit and image there  you can also list the contents of a  bucket or folder using the gsutil ls  command  here you can list the contents at the  top level of your bucket  the two items listed are kitten.png and  folder1  you can also list details for an  individual object using gsutil ls with  the dash l flag  here you can list details about kin.png  such as its size and date of creation  now let's explore how to manage cloud  storage permissions  run the gsutil iam ch command which  allows you to change permissions for a  given cloud storage bucket  here you specify that you want to give  all users permission to view objects  sorting the bucket  the command is successful if no error is  returned  to remove public view access to these  objects run the gsutil iam ch command  with the d flag  you may also want to give a specific  person access to your bucket  this command gives the gcp student  developer 7 at  account permission to read and write  objects to your bucket  to remove this permission run the same  gsutil iam ch command but with the d  flag  to delete an object run this gsutil rn  command to delete kin.png from the cloud  storage bucket  and finally run this command to delete  the cloud storage bucket  the dash r flag is added so that  everything inside of the bucket is  deleted as well  and that's it you're now equipped to  perform basic tasks in cloud storage  using the gsutil command line tool  while this video used cloud shell you  can also use gsutil from your local  computer  check out the gsutil getting started  guide linked in the description below to  learn how to install gsutil on your  local computer  make sure to check out the rest of our  cloud storage documentation at  cloud.google.com  storage  docs so you can learn more about how to  store and manage your data in google  cloud  [Music]
 

